<Base title="Roadmap — LevelSelect" description="Where LevelSelect is going: what's being worked on now, what's next, and what's being explored." current="roadmap">
<main class="wrap">
<article class="doc">
    <h1>Roadmap</h1>
    <p class="stamp">Last reviewed 17 August 2026</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>This is a direction, not a set of promises. Things move between sections, and some never ship at all. For what has actually shipped, see the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Now</h2>
    <p>Being worked on.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Settling the beta.</strong> Making the things people use every day dependable before adding more.</li>
      <li><strong>Trackers you can trust.</strong> Generating a tracker should never be a gamble with progress you've already made.</li>
      <li><strong>Mac polish.</strong> The Mac app works, but it doesn't yet feel as considered as the iPhone one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>Planned, not started.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Build a tracker piece by piece.</strong> Say what you actually care about — trophies, bosses, collectibles — and add categories one at a time instead of waiting on one big generation that can fail entirely.</li>
      <li><strong>Moments worth celebrating.</strong> Finishing a game, hitting a milestone, or completing a hundred small things should feel like something.</li>
      <li><strong>Replays.</strong> A look back at what you played over a month, a quarter or a year, built entirely from your own library on your own device.</li>
      <li><strong>Deeper stats.</strong> More ways to see your own playing habits — by year, by genre, by system.</li>
      <li><strong>A wider beta.</strong> Opening things up beyond the current small group.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Exploring</h2>
    <p>Ideas being thought about. No commitment, and some of these are genuinely hard.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Badges.</strong> Recognition for what you've actually done, earned once and kept.</li>
      <li><strong>Your own cover art.</strong> Swap a game's cover for something you prefer.</li>
      <li><strong>Scanning a written list.</strong> Point a camera at a checklist you wrote by hand and turn it into a tracker.</li>
      <li><strong>More ways to bring a library in</strong>, beyond CSV.</li>
      <li><strong>Making the app feel more like your own notebook</strong> — themes, icons, and the sort of personality a paper journal has.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not planned</h2>
    <p>Worth being clear about, since these are what the app is deliberately not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Accounts.</strong> Your library lives in your own iCloud. There's nothing to sign up for.</li>
      <li><strong>Ads, analytics or tracking.</strong> Not now, not later. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>A social network.</strong> Sharing something you choose to share is one thing; a feed is another.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</main>
